<Group width=100% height=100%>
    <ImportSettings><SiteTheme /></ImportSettings>
    <Stacker x={self.compact ? 24px : 6%} anchor_x=0% y=80px
        width={self.compact ? (100% - 48px) : 88%} autosize=true gutter=24px
        sizes={self.compact ? (@viewport.width < 760 ? [None, None, Some(370px)] : [None, None, Some(540px)]) : []}>
        ///#h2
        <Text class=["editorial_title", "modularity_copy"]>Plug, Extend, and Play</Text>
        <Text class=["editorial_lead", "modularity_copy"] markdown=true>Pax interfaces are built from reusable components, like layouts, native scrollers, text, buttons, and shader effects. Many of these are available in the [Pax standard library](https://docs.pax.dev/api/pax-std/index.html).</Text>
        ///#ignore
        <CircuitBoard @mount=self.bind_content_height layout_role={self.compact ? LayoutRole::Default : LayoutRole::Breakout}
            x={self.compact ? 0% : 52%} anchor_x=0% width={self.compact ? 100% : 48%}
            height={@viewport.width < 760 ? 370px : 540px}/>
        <Text class=["editorial_body", "modularity_copy"]>Connecting components together is like wiring pins together on a circuit board—predictable, modular, and deeply customizable.</Text>
        <Text class=["editorial_body", "modularity_copy"]>Your own components live in ordinary Rust modules, with Pax templates describing their composition. Build a widget, a layout, an effect, or an entire application section—and reuse it just like a standard-library component. 🐢</Text>
        <Text class=["editorial_body", "modularity_copy"] markdown=true>[Learn to build reusable components →](https://docs.pax.dev/components-composition.html#make-a-reusable-component)</Text>
    </Stacker>
    <Rectangle class="site_rule" x=50% y={100% - 1px} width={self.compact ? (100% - 48px) : 88%} height=1px/>
    <Rectangle class="site_ink" width=100% height=100%/>
</Group>

@settings {
    .modularity_copy {
        width: {self.compact ? 100% : 48%}
    }
    @mount: mount
}
